## What this tool does with Steinberg's software

Everything here works with a Dorico installation the user already has and has licensed:

- It speaks to the Remote Control WebSocket that Dorico exposes when the user turns it on in **Preferences >
  Remote Control**. That is a documented, user-enabled feature, used as intended.
- `file read` decodes `.dorico` files **the user owns**, reading them from disk. The format was reverse engineered
  for interoperability — to let a user's own scores be read by other tools — and the decoder is our own code.
- `tools/analyze_minidump.py` reads crash dumps of the user's own machine.
- Nothing here is a copy of Steinberg code, and no Steinberg binary, string table or other extracted data is
  distributed in this repository.

## Reverse engineering, and the vocabulary question

Commands, parameter names and enum spellings were learned by observing the application: its `application.log`
(which echoes every command Dorico performs), the command catalogue it publishes over the API, and — for the
unpublished surface — strings inside the executable on the maintainer's own machine.

The extracted vocabulary is **deliberately not** part of this repository. `tools/extract_vocabulary.py` reads a
locally installed Dorico executable and writes a per-user file under `%LOCALAPPDATA%\dorico-use\` for local
validation only; the list itself is never committed and never shipped. This is the same line the open-source
Dorico clients drawn on here have kept.

## Files you bring

Test fixtures named in `tests/` are files on the maintainer's machine. They are **not** in this repository. If you
want the full checks, point them at your own projects — see `CONTRIBUTING.md`, which explains how to add a score
you are allowed to redistribute so that continuous integration can run the real checks.
